# Response headers for the deployed site. DRAFT -- nothing is deployed.
#
# This is the `_headers` format read by Netlify and Cloudflare Pages: a plain
# file at the root of the published directory. web/build_dist.py copies it into
# dist/, so the headers travel with the build they protect instead of sitting
# in a repo-root config that can drift from a dist/ built separately.
#
# If the host turns out to be Vercel, this file is ignored and the same headers
# have to be restated in a vercel.json "headers" block. Say the word and it can
# be converted; the values below do not change, only the syntax around them.
#
# The CSP is the one web/serve.py already sends for local development, plus
# frame-ancestors. Keep the two in step: a header that only exists in
# production is a header nobody tests.
#
# frame-ancestors CANNOT be moved into a <meta http-equiv="Content-Security-
# Policy"> tag. The HTML spec requires user agents to ignore frame-ancestors,
# report-uri and sandbox when the policy arrives in a meta element, so a
# meta-only policy silently leaves the site framable. Clickjacking protection
# has to come from a real response header, which is why it is here and not in
# index.html.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
